millions of americans could be at risk  of eviction  in just two days when federal  protections created during the pandemic  are set to expire  about six and a half million households  were behind on rent earlier this month  and around half of those families and  individuals worry that  they could be evicted in the next two  months that's even though congress has  allocated more than 46 billion dollars  to help americans affected by the  pandemic pay their rent  janet chamleon is in las vegas where  just a fraction of the money for rent  relief has reached those  who need it most janet good morning  anthony good morning to you the rent  relief money is being allocated  differently state by state here in  nevada  they had only given out about three  million dollars by the end of june  texas on the other hand has doled out  some 400 million dollars  but even renters there are worried that  they're not going to get the help they  need  before the deadline the bumpus family  may be on a ramp to homelessness  jeremy says he was laid off in january  and hasn't paid rent in five months  when the moratorium ends saturday he's  worried his family will be evicted  i'm from california so i'm not from here  so i don't really have no backup plan or  no plan b  i don't have family and friends the  owner of their houston area home  has gone to court to get them out what  will it be like  at the end of the month when the  moratorium ends we are absolutely  terrified  of what will happen at the end of the  month once the moratorium ends because  any case that was on hold can no longer  be on hold  juan santa maria of lone star legal aid  says he has  60 more cases just like the bumpuses  protests have erupted across the country  as the eviction crisis has worsened  as many as 15 million people are in the  same situation as the bumpuses  at risk of losing their homes while they  wait  for federal rent relief the bad news is  that the money is getting out  very slowly getting the money quickly  this week diane yentl of the national  low-income housing coalition  offered congress a startling fact in  some places it's not getting out at  all and it will not reach all the  renters who need it to stay stably  housed in time  nearly half the states in the country  have used less than five percent of  their federal relief money  as of june florida south carolina and  new york  had given out less than one percent but  the money has been flowing faster  recently  deputy treasury secretary wali adayama  acknowledged that early rules  like allowing only landlords to apply  for relief  slowed down distribution we revised the  rule to make sure  that tenants individual people can  actually apply for that money  in addition to doing that we've  simplified the application process  and we've offered local communities and  states the opportunity to work with them  to make sure that we can improve the  ways that they get the money out the  door  and i really don't think we're going to  see this big wave of pending evictions  if anything else i think it's going to  cause people to really  incentivize that communication between  renter and property owner  john boryak owns these lower income  apartments in houston  and heads up an association of area  property owners  he believes the moratorium should not be  extended saying it's taken an especially  hard hit on so-called mom and pop owners  with only a handful of properties this  is their livelihood and so when those  rents go unpaid the mortgage is still  due and the utility bills are still due  advocates like santa maria say rent  relief programs  are working but require patience it's  understandable if you haven't been paid  in four  or five months you're not thinking the  six month or the seventh month  that's when you're going to get paid  these programs are successful it just  takes a little bit of time  king queen but the clock is ticking as  millions struggle to keep a roof  over their heads in some cases 